Keep the records that explain an agent’s actions, then check them against what was allowed. Today’s stories cover readable session history, tool-level evaluation, evidence coverage in AI log analysis, and two fixes for trustworthy trace delivery.

## Mainplane keeps agent session state in readable files on a separate server

Source: Hacker News · Alexander9 — https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50246690
Published: 2026-10-26
Channel: hn

Mainplane is an agent harness: software that coordinates model calls, tools and session state. In its first-party Show HN launch, developer Alexander describes keeping that coordination on a separate server while shell and file operations run on connected worker machines. Each session is stored in one append-only file, where new events are added at the end rather than rewriting earlier ones, which the developer presents as both recoverable state and an agent trace.

For illustration, an engineer investigating a failed task could inspect the session file on the server even if the laptop running a tool had disconnected. The design separates where actions happen from where their history lives. The announcement says a new session can search an earlier state file for context, rather than relying on a condensed summary. That is a described workflow, not an independently tested recovery guarantee.

The server holds model credentials and routes tool calls over a built-in private network that uses WireGuard, an encrypted VPN protocol. The developer also explicitly describes unrestricted agent access within the chosen worker computer. Readable session files therefore help investigation but do not establish permission enforcement, complete audit coverage or safe execution. This edition has not tested the harness.

Why it matters: For teams evaluating workstation agents: inspect session-file coverage and recovery behavior, and assess worker permissions separately from trace readability.

